1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benigna_Gottlieb_von_Trotha_gt_Treyden
2 CONT
2 CONT http://vesture.eu/index.php/B%C4%ABrona_Benigna_Gotl%C4%ABba_fon
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Бенигна Готлиб (1703-1782) Бирон, ур. фон Тротта-Трейден, герцогиня Курляндская с 1737 г., жена Э. И. Бирона, фрейлина двора Анны Иоанновны. После свержения Бирона сослана вместе с ним, возвращена Екатериной II в 1763 г.
2 CONT
2 CONT Герцогиня Бенигна Готтлиб, супруга герцога Эрнста-Иоганна (1703-1782) Дочь курляндского дворянина Вильгельма Тротта фон Трейдена. В 1720 г. стала фрейлиной при дворе герцогини курляндской Анны Иоанновны, а через три года вышла зам
2 CONC уж за Эрнста Иоганна Бирона. В Данциге в 1784 г. был изготовлен медный кованый саркофаг Бенигны Готтлиб (ум. в 1782).
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/167032770/benigna_gottliebe-von_trotta_genannt_treyden
2 CONT
2 CONT Benigna Gottliebe was the daughter of the Baltic nobleman Wilhelm von Trotta genannt Treyden (died in 1725) and his wife Anna Elisabeth von Wildemann. She was a lady-in-waiting to the dowager duchess regent of Courland, Anna of Ru
2 CONC ssia, in 1720, and in 1723 married to Anna's adviser Ernst Johann von Biron (Bühren). The marriage was reportedly arranged by Anna in an attempt to conceal her own relationship with Biron.
2 CONT Russia
2 CONT When Anna became Empress of Russia in 1730, the Biron couple both followed her to Russia, retaining their position as adviser and lady in waiting. Benigna is described as haughty and bad tempered and marked with smallpox, .She spe
2 CONC nt great amounts of money on clothes and jewelry and also received many costly gifts from Anna
2 CONT When her husband was made duke of Courland in 1737, she was decorated with the Order of Saint Catherine. She shared her husband's imprisonment in 1740 and returned with him to Courland in 1763, where she spent the rest of her life
2 CONC  at the court in Mitau (Jelgava).
2 CONT She is described as a good painter and textile artist, and she and her daughter embroidered a tapestry of silk which were displayed in the Jelgava Palace. She also published a book of religious German language poems, published in 
2 CONC Moscow in 1777 entitled "Eine große Kreuzträgerin".
